WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.420
Good morning, John. Top 10 week has turned out to be quite fun. I hope we do it again sometime.

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:08.240
Can you imagine breaking your arm and then stabbing someone with the bone?

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:11.740
But really, I think the exploding ant deserved his place at the top of that list,

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:17.680
but that got me thinking about the biggest and most awe-inspiring explosions of all time.

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:21.520
Number one: The very first large man-made explosion

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:24.160
happened in 1654 in the Dutch town of Delft

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:26.860
when a 30-ton store of gunpowder exploded.

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:30.780
It pretty much destroyed the entire town and kind of created the idea of a bomb.

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:33.800
But really, on the scale of things, not a very big explosion.

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:38.900
The second explosion I'm gonna talk about is the largest accidental explosion of all time.

00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:43.500
It happened outside of the Canadian port of Halifax when two boats crashed into each other.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:48.140
Unfortunately, one of the boats was carrying nothing but explosives for use in World War I.

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:51.060
It created a tsunami and killed over 2,000 people

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:54.780
and pretty much destroyed the entire town of Halifax, Nova Scotia.

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:58.680
Number three: The world's second largest explosion before the advent of nuclear weapons

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:01.800
was the intentional destruction of an island in the East River

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:03.960
so that they could open up the East River to shipping.

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:06.900
Unfortunately, that one was too long ago to have video footage of it, however,

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:09.860
there was a similar island destruction in British Columbia in the 1950s,

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:12.860
and we do have video of that. Yeah.

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:16.860
Explosion number four: The largest explosion before the advent of nuclear weapons

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:20.220
killed around 10,000 Germans during World War I.

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:23.440
So the British troops actually dug under the German lines

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:26.940
and laid down a huge amount of explosives.

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:30.180
The evening before the attack, the British general was quoted as saying,

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:33.400
"Gentlemen, tomorrow, we may not change history,

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:36.780
but we certainly will change geography."

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:38.800
And before we get to nuclear weapons, 'cause we're gonna get there,

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:43.020
let's talk about the largest non-nuclear weapon ever designed by the US.

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:47.040
The Massive Ordinance Air Blast bomb, or MOAB

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:49.420
is also sometimes called the "mother of all bombs."

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:52.620
It has roughly the explosive power of 12 tons of TNT

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:55.400
and can destroy a 10-city-block radius.

00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:57.940
And now's when we start making really big jumps.

00:01:57.940 --> 00:02:00.440
So we go from the MOAB which was a big bang,

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:04.520
to a much bigger bang, which is a very small nuclear weapon.

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:09.100
Fat Man and Little Boy were the only nuclear weapons actually used in warfare.

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:13.620
Dropped on Hiroshima and Nagasaki, they're roughly 1,000 times bigger than the MOAB.

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:17.860
Explosion number 7: The Ivy King was the largest fission bomb of all time.

00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:20.520
Roughly 30 times the size of the bomb dropped on Hiroshima.

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:21.980
There's something in my eye.

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:26.300
Explosion number 8: The Tunguska Event happened in 1908

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:29.020
and no one is quite sure what happened.

00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:34.520
But whatever it was had an explosive force larger than 1,000 Hiroshima bombs.

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:36.300
The most prominent theory was that it was a very small comet,

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:39.080
not because of atmospheric pressure and the heat of re-entry,

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:41.420
it actually exploded before it hit the ground.

00:02:41.420 --> 00:02:46.200
Explosion number 9: The Tsar Bomba was actually, probably, like

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:50.600
5 times bigger than the Tunguska Event, which, wow!!

00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:52.520
The Tsar Bomba with a 50 megaton equivalence is

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:55.240
roughly the size of 4,000 Hiroshima bombs.

00:02:55.240 --> 00:03:01.720
Explosion number 10: The largest explosion and fairly recent geological history of the Earth

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:05.760
was the volcanic eruption that left behind Yellowstone National Park.

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:10.440
For those of you who don't know, Yellowstone National Park, one of America's most prized natural wonders,

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:15.100
which is a mere 100 miles away from where I'm sitting right now, is actually a volcanic caldera.

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:18.900
The last time it erupted was 640,000 years ago.

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:23.980
And the force of that explosion was roughly 17,000 times that,

00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:29.620
not of the Hiroshima bomb, but of the 50 megaton Tsar Bomba.

00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:35.600
The scary part is geologists say Yellowstone is "due for another eruption."

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:36.740
(whine)

00:03:36.740 --> 00:03:39.480
Okay, I live - I live way too close to this thing.

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:41.380
So I hope I freaked you out!

00:03:41.380 --> 00:03:46.100
Can you imagine living in a time when there's actually a feasibility of global nuclear war?

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:48.660
I don't even live in the '50s and I wanna go build a bomb shelter.

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:51.500
If you missed the other two Top 10 videos, you can see them right now.

00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:52.800
I'm gonna put an annotation link up.

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:55.220
John, I will see you on Monday.

